<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("topless_rise") %>
<% constants = Lab::ToplessRiseReading %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% repo_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main" %>
<% record_url = "#{repo_url}/docs/design_record.md" %>
<% studies_url = "#{repo_url}/docs/studies" %>
<% readings_url = "#{repo_url}/docs/readings/topless-rise" %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "With a stack NAND on a metabolism tape", finding_path("paid-parts-assemble-deep-logic-on-a-stack-nand") %>,
  paid parts assembled XOR and EQU in a sixth of the worlds. That ladder tops out at EQU: a
  world that holds it has nowhere further to be paid for. The
  <%= link_to "topless study", "#{studies_url}/topless.md" %> replaces it by a ladder with no
  near top, every function of four inputs, each paid by its minimal NAND count, its depth.
  <strong>When depth is paid, does the deepest rung a soup holds keep rising late in a long
  run?</strong> That is the open-endedness question on this machine.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The parents are every meta-stack-arm child of the
  <%= link_to "Meta-stack sweep", experiment_path(constants::PARENTS.fetch("experiment")) %>,
  54 worlds from 18, each continued from its last stored world for another <%= epochs %>
  epochs, seed <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).first %>, keeping its metabolism tape and stack NAND.
  Three arms, every one on the four-input ladder:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>rise</strong>: every rung paid by its depth, at a reward of
    <%= number_with_delimiter constants::REWARD, delimiter: " " %>, chosen so no single rung up
    to the ladder's floor saturates a cell.</li>
  <li><strong>capped</strong>: the same, but any rung deeper than
    <%= constants::DEPTH_CAP %> NANDs is paid as one of depth <%= constants::DEPTH_CAP %>.
    Depth past the cap buys nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>none</strong>: nothing paid; the depth is still read.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong>, with every number fixed before a child was
  seeded: <%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-10-02, "Topless rise: does
  the deepest rung held keep rising when depth is paid?", DESIGN §1.3 item 18. The rules are
  Logic's: a settling window of
  <%= number_with_delimiter constants::SETTLING_WINDOW, delimiter: " " %> epochs, decile
  medians, and no pair with an extinct child in a test. A child <strong>rises late</strong>
  where its last-decile median depth, the deepest rung held by a tenth of the world, is at
  least <%= constants::RISE_STEP %> above the deepest its lineage reached before the second
  half: its fifth-decile median, its parent's depth at descent, and every depth it held
  <%= constants::PERSISTENCE_RUN %> samples running by then. Climbing back to where the
  parent stood is no rise. Two one-sided sign tests at
  p &lt; <%= constants::SIGN_TEST_LEVEL %>:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>H-rise</strong>, the rung-4 question (rise against none): the rise rule.</li>
  <li><strong>H-rise-paid</strong> (rise against capped): the rise rule. Does the climb need
    depth itself to pay?</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Each test is also read with the extinct pairs kept, and on the children of Meta-stack's 9
  deep children alone, a declared subgroup. Neither re-reading decides an outcome. The entry
  also locks <strong>H-rise-code</strong>, descriptive and tested nowhere: whether a late rise
  in depth comes with new code or with old code co-opted.
</p>

<% reading = @show.topless_rise %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.any? %>
  <p>
    <% reading.tests.each do |test| %>
      <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.hypothesis %> <%= test.outcome_label %></span>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.mostly_ceilinged? %>
      <strong>Most rise children are ceilinged: the ladder had a near top after all, and the
      tests are not read as an answer.</strong>
    <% elsif reading.climb_stopped? %>
      <strong>Paid for depth, the soup did not keep climbing.</strong>
    <% else %>
      <strong>Paid for depth, the deepest rung rose late: see the tests below.</strong>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.interim? %>
      Not every child of this sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <ul id="topless-rise-finding-tests">
    <% reading.readings.each do |test, kept, deep| %>
      <% comparison = test.comparison %>
      <% treatment = test.treatment.name %>
      <% control = test.control.name %>
      <li class="mb-3">
        <strong><%= test.hypothesis %></strong>, <%= treatment %> against <%= control %>:
        <%= comparison.favouring %> pairs favour <%= treatment %>, <%= comparison.against %>
        <%= control %> and <%= comparison.ties %> tie, of <%= comparison.measured_count %>
        measured<% if comparison.p_value %>, p = <%= p_value_text(comparison.p_value) %><% end %>,
        <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.outcome_label %></span>.
        <% if comparison.agreement.any? %>
          <%= pluralize(comparison.agreement.count { |row| row.treatment.positive? }, "parent") %>
          of <%= comparison.agreement.size %> gave a pair favouring <%= treatment %>.
        <% end %>
        <% [["With the extinct pairs kept", kept], ["On the deep parents' children", deep]].each do |label, other| %>
          <%= label %>: <%= other.comparison.favouring %> to <%= other.comparison.against %>
          with <%= other.comparison.ties %> ties<% if other.comparison.p_value %>,
          p = <%= p_value_text(other.comparison.p_value) %><% end %>,
          <span class="badge <%= other.badge_class %>"><%= other.outcome_label %></span>.
        <% end %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <% rise_risers = reading.late_risers(:rise) %>
  <% capped_risers = reading.late_risers(:capped) %>
  <p id="topless-rise-finding-risers">
    <%= pluralize(rise_risers.size, "rise child", plural: "rise children") %> of
    <%= reading.arm(:rise).children.size %> rose late<% if rise_risers.any? %>
    (<%= safe_join(rise_risers.map { |child| link_to("run #{child.run_id}", run_path(child.run_id)) }, ", ") %>)<% end %>,
    against <%= capped_risers.size %> capped and <%= reading.late_risers(:none).size %> none.
    Settled relapses: <%= reading.relapse_count(:rise) %> rise,
    <%= reading.relapse_count(:capped) %> capped and <%= reading.relapse_count(:none) %> none.
    <%= pluralize(reading.extinct_count, "child", plural: "children") %> died out and
    <%= reading.ceilinged_count %> reached the ladder's floor of
    <%= constants::CEILING_DEPTH %> before the second half.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count above is read from the sweep's stored samples at render time, through the same
    reading <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("topless-rise", anchor: "topless-rise-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/topless_rise_reading", reading: reading.report %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No child of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("topless-rise") %> is in this database,
    so there is nothing to read here. This page reads the children at render time and states
    the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>H-rise-code, measured offline</h2>
<p>
  The pre-registration locks a descriptive reading of the code behind each late rise: on the
  fifth-decile world and the last, the <strong>dominant deepest solver</strong>, the commonest
  metabolism tape credited the world's deepest solid rung, and its
  <strong>load-bearing bytes</strong>, the positions where at least
  <%= constants::CODE_BEARING_SYMBOLS %> of the 13 other symbols lose that depth on
  <%= constants::CODE_CASE_SETS %> fixed four-input case sets. Where the depth rose, the code is
  <strong>new code</strong> if the count rose by <%= constants::CODE_NEW_BYTES %> or more,
  <strong>co-option</strong> if it did not rise, and <strong>neither</strong> if it rose by 1.
  It was made on 2026-10-04 with <span class="mono">research/landscape/</span> at main
  <span class="mono">3a10d32</span> on the lab's stored worlds, read through SELECTs only, and
  every world was checked byte for byte against the lab. It is an offline measurement, not this
  page's code, so the table below is typed from its
  <%= link_to "summary", "#{readings_url}/summary.txt" %>
  (<%= link_to "one row per child", "#{readings_url}/summary.csv" %>), whose
  <%= link_to "README", "#{readings_url}/README.md" %> gives the scripts as run. The lock covers
  the one rise child that rose late and its twins; every other child was read too, as a
  descriptive extension. Every child resolves to the same three stored worlds: 62&nbsp;000,
  110&nbsp;000 and 160&nbsp;000.
</p>
<figure class="table-figure mt-4">
  <figcaption class="table-caption text-sm text-muted" id="topless-rise-finding-code">
    The label from the fifth-decile world to the last, all 54 children of each arm.
  </figcaption>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="topless-rise-finding-code">
    <table class="data-table" aria-labelledby="topless-rise-finding-code">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Arm</th>
          <th class="numeric">New code</th>
          <th class="numeric">Co-option</th>
          <th class="numeric">Neither</th>
          <th class="numeric">No rise in depth</th>
          <th class="numeric">Unread</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>rise</td><td class="numeric">3</td><td class="numeric">0</td><td class="numeric">1</td><td class="numeric">50</td><td class="numeric">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>capped</td><td class="numeric">6</td><td class="numeric">7</td><td class="numeric">0</td><td class="numeric">41</td><td class="numeric">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>none</td><td class="numeric">0</td><td class="numeric">0</td><td class="numeric">0</td><td class="numeric">45</td><td class="numeric">9</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</figure>
<figure class="table-figure mt-4">
  <figcaption class="table-caption text-sm text-muted" id="topless-rise-finding-locked">
    The locked set: depth, then load-bearing bytes, of the dominant deepest solver in each world.
  </figcaption>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="topless-rise-finding-locked">
    <table class="data-table" aria-labelledby="topless-rise-finding-locked">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Child</th>
          <th>Arm</th>
          <th class="numeric">62&nbsp;000</th>
          <th class="numeric">110&nbsp;000</th>
          <th class="numeric">160&nbsp;000</th>
          <th>Label</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5137</td><td>rise</td><td class="numeric">0 / 2</td><td class="numeric">7 / 13</td><td class="numeric">10 / 15</td><td>new code</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5138</td><td>capped</td><td class="numeric">0 / 4</td><td class="numeric">7 / 12</td><td class="numeric">7 / 12</td><td>no rise in depth</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5139</td><td>none</td><td class="numeric">0 / 9</td><td class="numeric">0 / 2</td><td class="numeric">0 / 2</td><td>no rise in depth</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</figure>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>The one rise child that rose late is a re-climb, not a climb.</strong> 5137, of
    parent 4971, relapsed early and climbed back to depth 10 with 2 more load-bearing bytes,
    new code by the rule's minimum margin. Depth 10 is the arm's common plateau: 38 of the 54
    rise children end at 10, and 36 of them were already at 10 in the fifth-decile world. 5137's
    last solver carries the same loop core as other rise children's depth-10 solvers.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The capped arm creeps, mostly one rung.</strong> Its late risers end at depth 7 or
    8, which the cap pays as depth <%= constants::DEPTH_CAP %>. The nine that end at 8, seven of
    them one rung up from 7, all carry one recurring 10-byte loop,
    <span class="mono">{,~[{~&gt;~!]</span> or a near variant of it. Counting only steps from
    depth 4 or more, 4 are new code, each +2 bytes, and 7 co-option. The parents are
    correlated, so the recurring loop may be shared ancestry rather than convergence.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>+2 bytes is barely above drift.</strong> Of the 91 children whose fifth-decile and
    last worlds hold the same depth above ECHO, only 2 gain 2 or more load-bearing bytes.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Deeper solvers are longer code, but the late steps do not grow it.</strong> Across
    all 486 worlds the median count is 10 at depth 7, 12 at depth 8 and 14 at depth 10. Depth
    gained late does not come with sustained code growth.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What it means</h2>
<p>
  <strong>The climb stops.</strong> Paid for depth, on a ladder with no near top, the soup takes
  the jumps a reorganisation offers early, to depth 10 in most rise children, and then stands
  there for the rest of a <%= epochs %>-epoch run. The one late rise is a lineage climbing back
  to that plateau. The capped arm, whose deeper rungs pay nothing extra, rose late more often
  than the rise arm, mostly by one rung onto a loop it shares across parents. Paid parts bought a deeper feature, not a climb.
  That is the open-endedness answer for this machine, and it is a negative one.
</p>
<p>
  It holds on a machine built for it. The substrate imports five things: <strong>an
  objective</strong>, the paid tasks; <strong>a primitive</strong>, the NAND byte;
  <strong>a hereditary channel</strong>, the metabolism tape the world copies;
  <strong>the primitive's semantics</strong>, the stack NAND; and <strong>a ladder</strong> of
  growing input arity.
</p>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Nothing about rung 4 on Soup.</strong> Whatever rose or did not here was paid for by
    tasks the programme chose. It is never pooled with the fitness-free runs, and rung 4 on
    Soup stays "not shown".
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not a ceiling of four inputs.</strong> No child held depth
    <%= constants::CEILING_DEPTH %>, the ladder's floor, in any sample: the soup stopped well
    below the deepest the reading can show.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not every budget.</strong> It is one substrate, one reward, one seed a parent and
    <%= epochs %> epochs, and the 54 parents are three correlated children of 18 worlds.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What comes next</h2>
<p>
  The next line removes the imports rather than paying for more, the ablation ladder of the
  <%= link_to "unassisted study", "#{studies_url}/unassisted.md" %>.
  <%= link_to_if @show.sweep_seeded?("out_compute"), "Out-compute", experiment_path(Lab.slug_for("out_compute")) %>
  (DESIGN §1.3 item 19) replaces the paid ladder by an endogenous rule, a cell taking energy
  from a neighbour whose every computed function it computes too.
  <%= link_to_if @show.sweep_seeded?("genes_rise"), "Genes rise", experiment_path(Lab.slug_for("genes_rise")) %>
  (DESIGN §1.3 item 20, pre-registered in <%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>,
  2026-10-04, "Genes rise: does held code keep rising late, at a cap the run does not reach,
  with nothing paid?") adds a growable metabolism channel run as independent genes, with a
  priced, heritable fidelity. The study's pilots (§8–§13) are honest about what that took: a
  late climb appeared only once the channel's length was out of reach, and a capped twin
  flattened within a few thousand epochs. Room to grow, not pay for depth, is what they climbed
  on.
</p>
